al PRD content, so I'll propose the cmd/version task while being transparent that I'm using generic descriptions and referencing prd011-magefiles as required reading for the stitch agent to map to the actual requirements. I'm realizing the use case is broader than just cmd/version—that's just the code generation target specified in DD7. Looking back at the constraint about not reimplementing existing packages, I need to check if there are already .go files for the relevant packages in the source code. Looking at the existing packages, they all seem fairly complete with their current functionality. I'm noticing the code_status shows \"none\" for codereadiness across all releases and \"not started\" for all use cases, which is interesting context for what I'm evaluating.\n\nThe architecture clarifies that generated Go code exists at the tagged releases from the first pipeline run, but the current working branch has no code present yet. Since the source code is provided to me as context and the constraint says not to recreate packages that already exist in the source, I shouldn't reimplement what's already been generated. This means the only new work needed is cmd/version.\n\nFor the cmd/version task, I need to decide whether to propose just the implementation or split it with tests. Since the guideline says to always split implementation and tests into separate tasks, and I can only propose one task right now, I'll focus on just the implementation file. The challenge is I don't have the specific PRD requirement IDs to reference, so I need to figure out how to handle that constraint.\n\nI think the best approach is to reference the PRD directly and describe what cmd/version needs to accomplish based on the magefiles requirements, then let the stitch agent align those with the specific PRD sections. I'll draft a task that includes the PRD as required reading and frames the requirements in functional terms rather than trying to guess at specific requirement IDs. I need to follow the established patterns from other cmd/ packages—using the SIGPIPE handler, manual flag parsing, and matching exit code conventions—while ensuring the version output format and any flag support align with what the PRD specifies. I'm realizing I've been too prescriptive about implementation details that should come directly from the PRD itself. The ldflags suggestion and the assumptions about --version and --help flags aren't grounded in what the PRD actually requires, so I should strip those out. My focus should be on ensuring the stitch agent reads the PRD thoroughly and implements according to its actual specifications, while relying on the project conventions I do know about. I need to implement cmd/version following the patterns established in cmd/cat and cmd/wc, which means using package main with a progName constant, installing the SIGPIPE handler from pkg/sys, and writing errors to stderr with the program name prefix. The version output format and exit codes must match what prd011-magefiles specifies, with success returning 0 and errors returning 1. I'm realizing I can't anchor to specific PRD requirement IDs without actually seeing the prd011-magefiles content, so maybe I shouldn't propose cmd/version at all if the PRD isn't available to me. Let me reconsider whether the project context alone is sufficient, or if missing PRD details means this work isn't ready for generation yet.\n\nLooking back at the planning constitution, the earliest incomplete release is 00.0 with only cmd/version remaining, so that's the priority regardless. I'll propose the task and acknowledge that I'm referencing prd011-magefiles for the stitch agent to read, using a generic requirement anchor since I don't have the specific IDs.\n\nNow I'm checking the constraints around code volume and file count. The cmd/version utility will be small—probably 30-50 lines in a single file—which falls short of the 150-250 line target, but that's expected for a trivial utility. I could exp